{
 "id": "lighting.indoor_practical",
 "label": "Practical light",
 "description": "Named scene light sources: lamps, flames, screens, signs, outdoor fixtures, and speculative luminous objects.",
 "pos": "noun phrase",
 "frame": "lit by ___",
 "notes": "Visible scene sources belong here, indoors or outdoors, phrased to follow “lit by.” lighting.natural owns sun, sky, and moonlight; lighting.studio owns photographic rigs, and lighting.dramatic owns designed cinematic effects. Era and genre tags describe source availability, including explicitly speculative emitters; gas lamps and other older fixtures remain available today. The space tag is the setting a source needs: a portable or freestanding source (a candle, a lamp, a screen, string lights) places nothing and is tagged any; a fixture that puts the scene somewhere (a streetlight, a fireplace, a vanity mirror, a stage) is tagged with its setting and is drawn only once the prompt or a drawn place states it (context.takes.space.unstated). Firelight means an open outdoor fire, while fireplace glow means an indoor hearth. Lightning is kept here as a brief scene event rather than steady ambient daylight.",
